WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.817
- Nature, the most powerful
creative force on earth.

00:00:06.817 --> 00:00:09.240
(intense orchestral music)

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.010
I'm Chef Melissa King.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.620
Cooking has taken me to incredible places.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.453
Magical.

00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:17.400
From TV competitions and celebrity galas

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.620
to countries around the world.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.320
I'm heading out to places
I've never been before

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.210
to seek out new experiences

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.570
and to create new dishes
inspired by nature.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.220
This is amazing!

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.860
I'm teaming up with some of
the world's greatest explorers

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.020
to go further than I've ever gone before.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.566
- [Kiliii] This
environment is challenging.

00:00:36.566 --> 00:00:39.940
- To push my craft,
creativity, and my cooking.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.990
This is Tasting Wild.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.740
(wind whistling)

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.610
Frozen landscapes fascinate me.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.200
That's why I've come to Mount Baker

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.590
in Washington's Snoqualmie
National Forest.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.440
The mountains are snow capped year round

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.070
and the land's been carved
by glaciers over millennia.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.920
I don't spend a lot of
time in cold climates,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.730
so I'm excited to be here
to push myself and my craft.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.870
Leading me on this journey

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.062
is National Geographic Photographer,

00:01:12.062 --> 00:01:13.145
Kiliii Yuyan.

00:01:14.361 --> 00:01:16.009
Hey, Kiliii.
- Hey there.

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:16.950
- How's it going?

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:17.880
- I'm so excited to meet you.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.900
- So great to meet you.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.130
So, where are we headed?

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.730
- We are headed up the mountain.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.770
And it's gonna be epic.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.640
- All right, let's go.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.080
- All right, let's do it.
- Put your stuff in the car.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.460
- [Kiliii] As the National
Geographic Photographer,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.120
it's my job to tell the
stories of the Far North

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.460
and the Indigenous Peoples who live in it.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.860
Hopefully, my photographs
help to move people

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.830
to return to connecting with the land

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.530
and remembering the
lessons that it has for us.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.140
And one of the best parts
of being a Nat Geo Explorer

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.890
is being asked to impart
knowledge and to inspire people.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.983
So I want to immerse Melissa in nature.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.700
- I've been cooking for 20 plus years

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.520
and I'm always discovering something new.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.400
And a lot of that has to do with nature,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.860
not just relying on your taste buds.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.100
It's relying on your sight, your smell,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.340
and really finding ways to
kind of add in those cues

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.570
into my food.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.500
- I love that.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.790
There's something really ancient

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.470
about taste and smell, right?

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.780
Like, you smell something
or you taste something,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.120
and it instantly transports
you back to that place

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.730
where you were when you last had that.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.670
- I'm excited to see what's
in this river over here.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.400
Trout and salmon and great river fish.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.390
- The Nooksack River,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.370
which we're paralleling right now,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.490
it starts with the snow up high,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.400
and then as that snow melts
and becomes a tiny stream,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.470
it gets bigger and
bigger as it comes down.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.310
All that water is the
lifeblood of this ecosystem.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.020
We're starting to get into these places

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.430
where I feel at home

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.380
and you already start to see the snow

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.660
and that's definitely what I love.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.610
- I'm ready for it.

00:02:56.473 --> 00:02:59.806
(soft orchestral music)

00:03:13.003 --> 00:03:14.220
Woo!

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.183
Wow.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.300
- This is pretty much my happy place.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.177
- It's my new happy place now.

00:03:20.177 --> 00:03:21.120
(laughing)

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.010
- This area actually holds the record

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.500
for the most snowfall
anywhere in the world

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.373
in a single year.

00:03:27.441 --> 00:03:29.310
- Wow.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.710
The air is just so crisp and clean here.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.770
You could like smell
the pine and the spruce.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.330
- You want to get geared up and head out?

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.353
- Yeah, let's go.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.550
As a chef, I'm here to
find inspiration in nature

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.350
and apply it to my craft of cooking.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.540
There's so much power in
this beautiful glacier.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.820
I want to create a dish
that represents everything

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:51.970
that we're seeing here.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.060
This is very different than
California, where I'm from.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.970
We're used to sunny beaches.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.754
(laughing)

00:04:00.754 --> 00:04:02.910
(wind whistling)

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.950
I'm curious what grows here.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.630
- Well, this kind of alpine environment

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.760
is a lot like the tundra of the arctic.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.750
Everything has to live

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.570
underneath this layer of
snow for eight months a year.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.512
Resilience.
- Just like the will power

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:15.345
and resilience of life.

00:04:15.345 --> 00:04:16.178
- Yeah.

00:04:16.178 --> 00:04:17.640
Spring comes, the snow melts,

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.070
and then just like, boom, here we go.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:20.903
- [Melissa] Wow.

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:21.900
- And they're reaching for the sky,

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.460
growing as fast as they can

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.550
'cause you can sort of see all
these little pockets of life

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.843
that are just coming out.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:29.850
Well, here we are.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.340
You can see where the
snow was all melted away

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.220
and it's pretty amazing.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.550
This is the alpine blueberry
and those are the flowers.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.683
- Wow, these are so cute.

00:04:40.683 --> 00:04:42.693
Like cute little garnishes.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.520
I'm gonna eat one.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:46.703
I can't resist.

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:48.440
- What does it taste like?

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.450
- It has like a little
sweetness from the nectar.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.030
I love sourcing things locally

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.660
and just foraging straight off the trees

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.290
and finding ways to really
tell a story through my food

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.520
because I don't know,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:03.870
it's my love language.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.770
That's how I think.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.880
It's how my brain works.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.640
- I also tell stories through the building

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.900
and crafting of things.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.330
And one of the things that I craft

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.150
and make by hand is something
we're actually gonna need

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.170
in just a moment.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.720
So yeah, why don't we
head on and check it out?

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.863
- Okay, I'm curious.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.160
- [Kiliii] One of the striking
things about this place is

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.650
this is where you can
literally see glacial ice

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.490
and water carving this land
into the forms that they see.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.643
Nature's a master craftsman
that forms the lakes here.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.758
- Yeah, I'm already tired.

00:05:36.758 --> 00:05:38.820
(laughing)

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.023
- And here it is.

00:05:40.023 --> 00:05:42.240
It's a magic spot.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.503
Upper Bagley Lake.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.170
- Wow.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.180
Yeah, what kind of fish
are hanging out down there?

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.910
- Well, there's brook trout
in some of these alpine lakes,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.010
so hopefully we'll be able to catch one.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.230
- I've never caught a fish before,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.700
but I'm excited to try.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.260
- [Kiliii] Yeah, right on, yeah.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:05:59.730
- So, how are we gonna get there?

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.470
- Well, we'll hike around,

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.410
but before we do, first
let's grab some kayaks.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.430
- Amazing.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.650
- [Kiliii] I am a National
Geographic Adventurer,

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.780
but I'm also a traditional kayak builder.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.330
Kayaks are traditional
from my Nanai culture,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.310
so I spent a lot of my teenage years

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.040
and early twenties learning
to build the kayaks

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:17.940
not only of my people,

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.460
but different communities
across the Arctic,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.610
which led me on the road to finding elders

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.050
where I was able to learn from them

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.053
because I'm holding onto an
entire culture's traditions.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.650
- The kayak smells so good.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:35.490
What is it made of?

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.323
- Oh, yeah!

00:06:36.323 --> 00:06:38.850
So the internal frame
is western red cedar.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.290
- That's what it is.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.640
- These are all handmade

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.330
and this is a millennia
old Indigenous technology.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.430
To this day, there are no kayaks as light

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:48.990
and as strong as these.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:49.823
These joints,

00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:50.880
they're actually tied together

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.530
so that they have some movement

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.450
which allows the joints to flex.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:55.680
Let's get 'em in the water.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.120
- So excited.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.920
I've kayaked a couple times in my life,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.443
but never like this.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.793
The water is incredibly calm.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:07.953
It's so still.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.913
There really is a
peacefulness to this place.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.530
- [Kiliii] Traditional kayaks are special

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.430
in that you're not as insulated
from the water itself.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:22.710
I hope that Melissa get
the chance to feel that

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.150
because the kayaks flex,

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.693
she can feel the water and
even the cold through her legs.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.530
- As a chef, we tend to
get stuck in kitchens.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.880
I love being outdoors.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.517
I think that's, for me, a
way that I find inspiration

00:07:35.517 --> 00:07:39.063
and a way that I can connect
deeply with what's around me.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.030
You're a good teacher.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.190
You gotta teach me how to
make one of these boats.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.140
- Building kayaks is a craft,

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.413
but absolutely what you
do is absolutely craft.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:52.380
- It's similar, I think.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.380
I'm sure with you, with your boats,

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.760
is it ever perfect?

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.680
- No, it's never perfect,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.660
but I actually kind of love that too.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.580
- You're always learning new techniques,

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.733
new ways to make a dish
better or make a boat better.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.770
I want to find a way to utilize
what's here in these lakes

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.063
to create my dish.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.393
Kiliii said there was brook trout

00:08:14.393 --> 00:08:16.383
and I'm really hoping to catch one.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.126
Did you catch anything?

00:08:20.126 --> 00:08:22.950
(laughing)

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.410
The fish aren't really biting,

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.660
so I'm going to pick up
some locally sourced trout

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.360
in the morning before we head out.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.350
Thank you so much for taking
me on that adventure yesterday.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:44.850
It's the coolest thing I've ever done.

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.110
- No, of course.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.550
It was super fun for me too.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.470
- I foraged for a few ingredients

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.900
and I'm really excited to cook you dinner.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:52.950
- [Kiliii] All right.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.150
- [Melissa] I found a really cool place

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.130
up on the top of the mountain
that I'm gonna take you to.

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:57.080
- [Kiliii] Awesome!

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.060
- I hope it works for you.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.110
- Yeah, that's amazing.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.130
Look at that.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:05.963
Woohoo.

00:09:05.963 --> 00:09:07.380
From here, there's a vantage point

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:08.820
where you can see Mount Shuksan

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.770
and there's ice breaking off the glacier

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:12.320
and tumbling down the mountain.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.500
- I hope you're hungry.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:17.460
- Definitely.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.903
- Let's set up shop here.

00:09:19.921 --> 00:09:21.270
All right, so Kiliii,

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.370
why don't you make
yourself comfortable here?

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.220
I'm gonna set up the prep
station and also start the fire.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.356
- Cool.

00:09:27.356 --> 00:09:28.343
I'm gonna grab my camera actually.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.213
- [Melissa] When I cook,

00:09:32.213 --> 00:09:34.980
I want to transport you to another place,

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:36.990
take inspiration from what's around me,

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.333
and hopefully tell a
story through that dish.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.000
- All right, put me to work.
- Yeah?

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.980
We have some spruce that we foraged.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.440
I'm gonna have you chop that up.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:47.640
Do you have a knife?

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.473
- Sure do.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:50.905
- That's awesome.

00:09:50.905 --> 00:09:52.473
Okay, you're ready to go.

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:55.170
What's the story behind your knife?

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:56.550
- Well, this is a traditional knife

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.550
belonging to one of our
neighboring tribes actually,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.100
and they're really
famous for knife making.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.110
Yeah, it's reindeer antler.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:05.250
- [Melissa] What do you use it for?

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.920
- I use it mostly for
building kayaks actually.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:08.753
- [Melissa] Oh really?

00:10:08.753 --> 00:10:11.160
- [Kiliii] It's a great
all-purpose hunting knife.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.134
- Your knife and my knife
look very different.

00:10:13.134 --> 00:10:14.400
(laughing)

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.780
We have this beautiful trout,

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.660
some spruce needles, salmon berries.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.210
These could go really
nicely inside the trout.

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.670
I'm also gonna throw some lemon slices.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.650
- [Kiliii] That is just beautiful.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.470
- I thought about salt
baking it 'cause to me,

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.630
that represents the glacier,

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.340
all of that snow that we saw.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.190
And so here's our mound of salt.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:38.100
Have egg whites to bind it all together

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.960
along with those different
types of pine needles

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.270
that we found around the area.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.750
The pine salt reminds me of all that life

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.300
that was really growing
underneath the snow.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.610
And we're gonna bury this trout right now.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.650
I know we didn't catch
this fish yesterday,

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.540
but we still are working with some trout

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.640
because that's what's in these waters.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.680
We wanted to bring that to the table.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.980
- Well, that's why they call
it fishing, not catching.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.330
- True.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.030
So we're gonna hide that
trout beneath the snow.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.140
- [Kiliii] Cool.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.120
- A little trout glacier.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.520
It's gonna be like slow cooking.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:12.353
And when we were up there,

00:11:12.353 --> 00:11:15.060
there with such an element of time

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.673
and everything moving slow and evolving.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:21.150
Rather than just grilling the fish,

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.200
I thought why don't we just slow bake it?

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.060
When you slow bake a fish with salt,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.870
you're really sealing and
trapping those flavors inside.

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.850
Also gonna brew us some tea.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.952
- Ooh, all right.
- Yeah.

00:11:34.952 --> 00:11:35.785
- [Kiliii] Nice.

00:11:35.785 --> 00:11:36.618
- We're gonna do some pine tea

00:11:36.618 --> 00:11:39.480
with some of those berries
that we've gathered.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.970
This trout is gonna take
a little while to cook,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:44.793
so we have some time to take
in this incredible view.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.040
It's pretty epic to be up here

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.530
with a National Geographic Photographer.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.230
